      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to assess reproducibility and validity of the 6 minute walk test (6MWT) in obese youth and to develop an equation to predict aerobic power (VO(2max))."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Fifty-two girls and 45 boys, ages 7-17.8 participated in the study. Three walk tests were administered 1 week apart. Each visit corresponded to a specific test, either an incremental treadmill test, or the 6MWT. The 6MWT was performed on two occasions 1 week apart to assess reproducibility. Treadmill and walk tests were randomly assigned to avoid order effect."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Subjects walked 689.4\u2009±\u200998.6 m on the initial 6MWT and 690.4\u2009±\u200998.2 m on the second test. Intraclass correlation coefficient was r = 0.99. The Bland and Altman method also showed good agreement between the two 6MWTs. Stepwise multiple regression determined that distance walked in 6 MWT and BMI are the most significant factors in predicting VO(2max). The prediction equation is VO2 max (ml.kg.min(-1)) = 26.9 + 0.014\u2009×\u20096 MWT (meters) - 0.38 × BMI (kg/m((2)))."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "In obese youth, the 6 MWT is a reproducible and valid test for assessing aerobic power. A prediction equation was developed to estimate VO(2)max, using distance walked in 6MWT and BMI. [Box: see text]."
        }
      ]},
